WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:08.000
THE COMMON SECURITY
AND DEFENCE POLICY

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.180
This is a European warship
conducting security operations in this area.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:31.060
If you observe any suspicious or illegal activity
or require assistance, call on channel 16.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.170
It's the early 2000s

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:48.190
and Somali pirates operating in the Gulf of Aden
are terrorising international shipping

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.110
and jeopardising
the supply of vital food aid to Somalia.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.180
The increasing threat at sea
combined with the humanitarian disaster on land

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.200
places the EU in a tough position:
to act or not to act.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.100
Intensive consultation
at national and European level

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.090
results in EU NAVFOR Somalia
Operation Atalanta,

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:17.060
the first naval mission
ever undertaken by the EU.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.240
The main challenges were,
of course,

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:26.100
embracing the European Union's
first maritime mission

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.030
against a very difficult adversary

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.000
and how to bring that together
a very long way from Europe

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.030
and sustain that operation
through a considerable period of time.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.160
One aspect of EU NAVFOR's mandate
is to protect vulnerable ships and vessels

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.240
carrying humanitarian aid
from pirate attacks.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.170
But with up to 5,000 ships a month
transiting the Gulf of Aden

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.140
and an area of operations
one-and-a-half times the size of Europe,

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.020
this is no easy task.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.160
The key to success is teamwork.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.040
We work very closely in partnership
with NATO who have a force out there

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.040
and the Combined Maritime Force
based in Bahrain.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.040
It's been one of the most interesting operations

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.050
in that it has brought exceptional cooperation,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:21.070
the sort of cooperation I don't think could happen
on land but has been available to us at sea.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.150
With several warships operating together
under the EU NAVFOR flag,

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.190
the number of attacks has reduced dramatically.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:37.120
Atalanta is probably
the most outstanding success story of CSDP,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.080
but fighting pirates at sea is not enough.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.060
We have to do something about the root causes.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:48.020
People become pirates not because it's romantic
but because it's a good business,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.070
so we need to open new perspectives
for the country.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.140
With tactical success at sea,
the next challenge,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.130
together with the African Union's
Mission to Somalia,

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:05.040
is to strengthen Somalia's embryonic army
to counter the threat of Al-Shabaab.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.160
A huge task for the EU military
training mission for Somalia.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:19.220
When in 2007 the African Union conceived
AMISOM as a peace mission for Somalia,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.010
few people came knocking on the door.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.040
Only the European Union believed in it

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.170
and invested in it
through the EUTM training programme,

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:33.090
which has trained
more than 3,000 Somali fighters for us.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:37.190
This is a country that has been
without a functioning state for 22 years,

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.190
so we should not expect
that things would be easy.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.100
But we have a new momentum
and energy to move forward.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:50.070
The EU Training Mission
was outside Somalia before.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.070
Now they are relocated in Somalia

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:57.040
and they are going to train the Somalis
inside Somalia.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.050
In a region devastated
by two decades of civil war,

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.210
security needs are as pressing
as social and economic ones.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.110
So the only effective response
is a comprehensive approach.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:13.150
It was very obvious we needed
a cohesive, coherent approach

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:18.190
if we were going to help the Somali people
realise their ambitions.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:23.130
Since then we've brought the full support
of the European Union

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:26.060
to Somalia and to the wider region.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:33.090
And I want to assure the Somalian people
that we will stand with them for the future.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:39.220
What's important to understand is
the European Union's ability

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:45.000
to begin to amalgamate
the various strengths it has.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.120
These are humanitarian, it is developmental,
it is now security.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:55.050
This is the Union beginning to give clout
to what it claims to be.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.190
Building on the successes of EU NAVFOR
and the training mission to Somalia,

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:10.100
in 2012 the EU launches EUCAP Nestor,

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:14.190
the first capacity-building mission
with a truly regional approach.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:18.180
It's to allow five countries
in the region of the Horn of Africa

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:25.170
to control their maritime areas
in order to prevent piracy in particular.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:29.030
It's to bring in laws,
and also to work in the maritime area,

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:35.190
i.e. to develop coastguard services or navies
that are able to control this area fully.

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:40.170
These are the tracks. These are
the different ships passing through the corridor.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:44.020
Frédéric Pasquier
is an experienced French gendarme

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.120
embedded in the Djiboutian
coastguard.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.150
His task is to help train this incipient force.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:54.140
They still need training, equipment,
practical experience.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.200
The European Union can share know-how,

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.180
while taking into account their own needs
and their own way of working.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.240
Like the coastguard,
the navy is also in strong need of support.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.060
Today the arrival of the Spanish warship Meteoro

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:14.150
provides the best platform
for a joint navy / coastguard diving exercise.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:21.100
Now we are going to review
the diving equipment that we have on board.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.170
Every time when a ship
goes to a jetty or to a pier

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:31.240
we inspect the jetty itself
to see if there is any suspicious object

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:35.050
and then if the ship is in harbour
we inspect also the hull

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.080
in order to see if there is
any threat to the hull or any damage.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:45.120
Another important task of EUCAP Nestor

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.230
is to help the countries in the Horn of Africa
strengthen their laws

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.060
to be able to fight piracy
and other crimes on the high seas,

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.030
such as illegal fisheries and trafficking.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.140
We are working in post-conflict societies

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.070
and one of the first priorities
is to make police and justice work again.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:07.150
In the aftermath of the Balkan wars,

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:12.000
Member States realised the need
for the EU to act as a security provider,

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:17.070
laying the foundations for Europe's
Common Security and Defence Policy.

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.070
CSDP was born out of frustration.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:26.070
We saw that we were not effective enough.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:32.040
At the same time it was our backyard.
So that was the realisation in the mid-'90s.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.040
And then it gave a very important impulse
to the development of a new policy.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:43.180
That was then written down in the treaties
and implemented as of 2003 in the field.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:48.100
A decade later,
the EU's Common Security and Defence Policy

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:50.180
has a proven track record of success

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.200
in both military operations
and civilian crisis management.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:00.080
The challenge now is to sustain this position
in the light of changing threats

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.150
and shrinking budgets.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:05.230
If you look at all the operations
over the last 10 years,

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:11.150
you can see that the Europeans, the armed forces
of the EU countries, are extremely present.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:17.060
But for all that, the situation which we are now in
is forcing us to go further.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.240
If Europe is to reap the full benefit

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:26.010
of its Common Defence and Security Policy,

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:33.040
it's also necessary that Europeans invest
sufficiently in robust military capabilities.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:37.020
We have our limits in terms of sustainability,

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:40.200
in terms of complexity of military operations.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:44.210
That's why we try to improve our capabilities,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.130
particularly in the area of air-to-air refuelling,
of strategic airlifts.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:54.160
By pooling and sharing our resources
in terms of material, people,

00:08:54.200 --> 00:09:00.010
EU Battlegroups and the specific niches
where we used to be lacking,

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:02.090
we will make a huge leap forward.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.090
We spend 200 billion euros a year
on defence in the European Union.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:11.130
So if I turn that into a challenge
and an opportunity,

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:15.130
I think we really have to do more now
to try and work collectively.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:20.210
Strengthening Europe's military muscle
by pooling and sharing key resources

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.050
may also boost its economy.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:25.180
Defence is undeniably a motor for growth.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:30.100
It also creates highly-skilled jobs.

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:34.170
So the defence sector is often responsible
for technological innovations

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:37.160
which support a whole economic sector.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:43.140
In maximising synergies between civilian
and military operations in the Horn of Africa,

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:49.060
the EU has contributed positively to the stability
of a region ravaged by war and poverty.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:52.170
But in the face of increasing
security challenges in the world,

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:58.050
how will the EU's role as a provider
of security evolve in the future?

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:01.140
We will be wherever we'll be needed.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.070
CSDP is demand-driven.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:09.030
We engage in countries and regions
where we can make a difference,

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:12.090
but of course we have to be
well-prepared to react.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:15.150
The potential is there.
We just need to make sure we can use it.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:42.010
European Union
EXTERNAL ACTION

